<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Aviz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Aviz was the royal dynasty of Portugal from 1385 to 1580, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> I. Under its rule, notably with the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, Portugal began its overseas expansion along the African coast. It thus presided over the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz.jpg" alt="House of Aviz" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stemma_della_famiglia_Aviz.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The founder, King John I, was born in 1357, the illegitimate child of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a> and Teresa Lourenço, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> merchant's daughter. In 1364 he was made Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-aviz">Order of Aviz</a> and became known as John of Aviz.</p><p>The dynasty arose from the crisis after the death of Ferdinand I in 1383. His designated heir was his daughter Beatrice, married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>, who claimed the throne in her name. In April 1385, amid popular revolt and civil war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> declared John, Master of Aviz, king. He was backed by the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, while the nobility split, most favouring Beatrice. His rule was secured by the victory at Aljubarrota on 14 August 1385; formal peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was signed only in 1411.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>To mark the victory John founded the Monastery of Santa Maria da Vitória, known as Batalha, whose chapel became the burial place of the dynasty's princes. Under the house Portugal became a world power. The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1415 was its first colonial venture, followed by exploration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 belongs to the period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> reached its zenith under Manuel I, and its decline began under John III.</p><p>Sebastian I, John III's grandson, who succeeded in 1557, died childless at 24 at the Battle of Alcácer Quibir. His great-uncle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-king-of-portugal">Cardinal-King Henry</a>, died two years later, also childless. Catherine, Duchess of Braganza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-prior-of-crato">António, Prior of Crato</a>, claimed the throne. António was acclaimed king in 1580, twenty days before Philip invaded Portugal and defeated his supporters at the Battle of Alcântara. Historians dispute whether those twenty days count as a reign. António held out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> until 1583.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Notable members include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, Peter, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, Eleanor of Portugal, Holy Roman Empress, and Isabella of Portugal, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. John I added the green Cross of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-aviz">Order of Aviz</a> to the arms of Portugal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> became a royal badge under Manuel I. Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> shared a monarch until 1640, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a> was proclaimed John IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Aviz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cortes voted taxes and acted at moments of crisis, notably at Coimbra in 1385, when the jurist João das Regras argued for the election of John I, founder of the <b>House of Aviz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It had a long Atlantic coastline, a tradition of maritime commerce and less farmland than Castile and Aragon, and under the <b>House of Aviz</b> it came to see oceanic expansion as the principal way to gain wealth and power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
